1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,745
Lesson 19: loop Yagi for 1296 MHz

2
00:00:04,795 --> 00:00:22,256
YA-NEC course, lesson 19: the loop Yagi for 1296 MHz. Calculators, Loop Yagi 1296 MHz.

3
00:00:22,306 --> 00:00:35,954
The N6CA design from the ARRL tables, the classic loop Yagi of G3JVL: 25 elements - two reflectors, the driven loop and 22 directors - on a 1.78 m boom, 7.7 wavelengths.

4
00:00:36,004 --> 00:00:53,738
The ARRL says about 18 dBi for 6 feet. Real time: 18.6 dBi, F/B 20.6 dB, 20.7 x 21 degrees. But the SWR minimum is at 1271 MHz, 25 MHz low.

5
00:00:53,788 --> 00:01:09,808
'Create the NEC model and check it'.

6
00:01:09,858 --> 00:01:24,051
400 wires: every loop is a 16-sided polygon of strip. At 1296.1 MHz: 50 + j9.6 ohm, SWR 1.21, 18.60 dBi.

7
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:29,115
Frequency tab.

8
00:01:29,165 --> 00:01:43,074
Gain almost flat from 1280 to 1310 MHz, F/B peak near 1300, but the SWR dip falls around 1290: the driven loop is a bit long.

9
00:01:43,124 --> 00:01:53,772
Back to the calculator: 'Optimize up to step' 1 - driven loop only.

10
00:01:53,822 --> 00:02:06,842
'Optimize (NEC)'.

11
00:02:06,892 --> 00:02:21,114
Seconds later: 48.1 - j0.9 ohm, SWR minimum 1.04 at 1296.8 MHz, gain 18.59 dBi. The directors are untouched.

12
00:02:21,164 --> 00:02:32,714
Steps 2 to 4 also tune reflectors and directors, as G3JVL does on the bench. The warnings recall the limits: no boom in the NEC model, strip loops as polygons.

13
00:02:32,764 --> 00:02:43,218
Loop Yagi: 400 wires, but the method is the same. Next lesson: satellite antennas - Eggbeater, QFH, Quagi. 73 de IC8TEM
